+| `11aa54c8` (branch `metalsim-cuda-fast-math`, cherry-picked onto `c1bb061b`) | `fast_math` modules on Metal can reproduce CUDA's `--use_fast_math` results bit for bit: `warp.config.metal_fast_math` (`WP_METAL_FAST_MATH`) = `"cuda"` / `"relaxed"` (the `f52da34` lowering) / `"off"` (fast_math ignored; the default since the next row). Measured on the L4 (Warp 1.16): a fast_math `/` is `div.approx.ftz.f32` = `MUFU.RCP` + `FMUL.FTZ`, `sqrt` is `MUFU.SQRT`, both exponent-invariant; `metal_cuda_fast_math.h` emulates both exactly in 32-bit integer arithmetic (Oberman-Siu interpolator with sass-math's tables, MIT, `licenses/sass-math-LICENSE.txt`), equal to the L4 on every float32 in [1, 2) / [1, 4), 2^22 random divisions and 16 Warp compositions; library divisions (vec / mat / quat element-wise, `normalize`, `inverse`) go through `WP_FDIV`. The mode is resolved with the module options (part of the hash of fast_math modules). Cost per op ~3x a correctly rounded division (div 10.8 ps, sqrt 13.6 ps vs 3.7 / 2.7 precise); Newton MPM 1.2-2.2x the `"off"` ms/frame. `"off"` / `"relaxed"` and non-fast_math modules are bitwise unchanged (six MPM scenes). Tests: `TestMetal.test_fast_math_approximates_division_and_sqrt_only`, `test_fast_math_cuda_mode_matches_l4`. |
